A||N||5||Monkeyman||Sub box questions||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||10-14-2001||09:19 AM||||I'm considering building a ported sub box in my front trunk (see this is Fiero related).  My sub needs 1.62 cu ft of volume in a ported box.  How many cubic inches are in a cubic foot?  (Sounds like a stupid question, but math wasn't my best subject.)<P>The specs also call for a certain length and diameter for the port.  What will change if the port tube is longer (or shorter)?  Will it matter if the port tube is bent?  Or does it have to be straight?  And what's this I've read about needing the ends of the port tube to be curled or beveled (something like that)?<P>Last, how does something like this sound compared to a firewall mounted sub or Aus' sub box? <P>I inquired (spelling?) about one for sale in The Mall, but haven't received any response yet so I might have to build my own.  It doesn't seem like it could be terribly difficult as long as there was sufficient volume. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PontiacAPE.jpg">    <BR>Monkeyman||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||10-14-2001||09:37 AM||||Hey MM!<BR>1 cubic foot=1,728 cubic inches...<BR>12x12x12...<BR>Do you already have a sub?<BR>I built a sub enclosure using 8 inch PVC tubeing and plexiglass...<BR>My 8 inch sub only needed .65 cubic foot, and it tucks up into the dash on the passenger side...<BR>Cranked up, it kicks ass! And down low it adds the basement to the music...<BR>Course, it also depends on the amp and filters you use...<BR>check out <A HREF="http://www.partsexpress.com" TARGET=_blank>www.partsexpress.com</A>  for ideas and parts!<P>BTW, how are you planning on ducting it into the pass compartment?<BR>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">          <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||10-14-2001||10:07 AM||||Cubic measures are in 3 dimensions. So if you just go by Length times width times depth, You get the total cubic inches. (See example in Fierowreckers post)<BR>As for changing port sizes, If the port is suppose to be 1" round and 4" long, You can change it to a 2" by 2". You can bend/angle the port 90* if need be. The ends being beveled/curved is for mounting purposes.<P>The advantages of a ported box are:<BR>Less power require for good sound.<BR>Get lower frequecies out (thru the port)<BR>Get lower frequecies out of smaller woofer sizes. <BR>The disadvantages are:<BR>Under high power distortion level is lower.<BR>(Depends on box design and quality of speaker)<BR>Usually a larger box is required for ported.<BR>Generally about 33% bigger (average)<P>A ported box will usually have more "boom" where a sealed box will have a sharper sound and require more power. <P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||10-15-2001||05:54 AM||||Thanx, guys.  I believe I'll start removing stuff from my front compartment today.  I'll see what sizes and shapes I can come up with.  What can I use for a port tube?  Does it absolutely have to be a commercially available part (such as from Crutchfield)?  I think theirs are straight and I would imagine that I'll have to have something bent.  As long as it's smooth, would it work?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||10-15-2001||06:57 AM||||i've heard curving the ports at the ends cuts down on wind noise of air moving in/out the port. turbulence.<P>jl audio makes some flexable tubing, looks like flexable exaust tubing only plastic i think, for ports.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||10-15-2001||07:00 AM||||Hmm...I'll have to check that out if I decide to go that way.  Thanx for the heads up.||||reg||1
